rpshow -- show a local file on a Raspberry Pi screen
====================================================

rpshow copies a file from this machine to a Raspberry Pi over ssh and
shows it in less, in a fullscreen terminal window on the Pi's own
monitor.  It is meant for a Pi that has a screen and a mouse but no
keyboard: the mouse wheel scrolls, and a right click closes the window
completely (the terminal, less and the remote copy of the file).

    rpshow notes.txt
    rpshow -w -H otherpi -d :0 report.md
    rpshow paper.pdf

Markdown files are rendered first, the way the rich module's
"python3 -m rich.markdown -c -p FILE" shows them in a local terminal;
see "Markdown" below.  PDF files are shown in zathura instead of less,
recoloured dark the way your zathurarc says; see "PDF" below.

Requirements
------------

On this machine: a C compiler and ssh, with key-based login to the Pi
(the default destination is the ssh host "pi"; see ~/.ssh/config).

On the Pi: an X session on the chosen display, alacritty 0.13 or later,
less, setsid and mktemp; lesspipe is used when present.  The window
settings are passed as TOML overrides ("-o mouse.bindings=..."), so
older alacritty releases with YAML configuration do not work.
Markdown rendering also needs python3 with the rich module
(python3-rich on Raspberry Pi OS and Debian).  PDFs need zathura 0.4.7
or later with its PDF plugin (zathura and zathura-pdf-poppler), but not
alacritty.

Markdown
--------

A file whose name ends in .md, .markdown, .mdown, .mkd, .mkdn or .mdwn,
in any case, is shown rendered: headings, emphasis, lists, tables,
quotes and highlighted code blocks, coloured and wrapped to the width
of the window.  The window runs

    PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 python3 -m rich.markdown -c FILE | less -R

on the Pi.  This is the same rendering as "python3 -m rich.markdown -c
-p FILE" (-c forces colour, -p pages the output); rpshow pipes into
less itself rather than letting rich pick a pager, so the less options
above still apply, and adds -R so the colours show.  Rendering happens
inside the window because rich wraps text to the terminal's width,
which only the Pi knows.  PYTHONIOENCODING makes rich write UTF-8 even
when the Pi's locale is not UTF-8.  Older rich releases render less:
9.11, the version in Raspberry Pi OS 11, shows tables as source text.

If the Pi's python3 cannot import rich, rpshow prints a note and shows
the Markdown source.  --raw (-r) shows the source on purpose.

PDF
---

A file whose name ends in .pdf, in any case, or whose first bytes are
"%PDF-" is shown in zathura, fullscreen and without status or input
bar; the mouse wheel scrolls and a right click closes it.  A PDF found
by its first bytes alone gets ".pdf" appended to its name on the Pi.
The window runs

    zathura --mode=fullscreen --config-dir=DIR --data-dir=DIR
            --cache-dir=DIR FILE

where DIR is the temporary directory holding the copy, so the Pi's
own zathurarc is not read and no history of the file is kept.

The pages are recoloured dark, as with "set recolor true".  The colours
come from this machine's zathurarc ($XDG_CONFIG_HOME/zathura/zathurarc,
or else ~/.config/zathura/zathurarc).  Only "set" lines for adjust-open,
default-bg, default-fg, recolor, recolor-darkcolor, recolor-keephue,
recolor-lightcolor, recolor-reverse-video, render-loading,
render-loading-bg and render-loading-fg are passed on, and only with a
value made of letters, digits, spaces and #(),.%+- (such as
rgba(0,0,0,1) or a quoted "#000000"); other lines, mappings and
include are ignored, so the file cannot make the Pi run anything.  A
"set recolor false" there turns the recolouring off; without a
zathurarc the PDF is recoloured with zathura's default colours.
--light (-l) shows the pages in their own colours.  With -v, the
warnings zathura prints in its first second (such as an option the
Pi's older zathura does not know) are reported.

If the Pi has no zathura, rpshow prints a note and shows the PDF as
text in less, which is what --raw (-r) does on purpose: lesspipe runs
pdftotext on it.

less options
------------

less on the Pi gets the options in $RPSHOW_LESS, or else this machine's
$LESS, so it behaves the way less does here.  Options the Pi's less does
not know are dropped (it has no keyboard to dismiss the error prompt),
and so is --mouse, which would take the right click away from the
terminal.  Run with -v to see what was dropped.

How it works
------------

rpshow opens FILE, checks that it is a regular file and runs ssh with
that file as its standard input.  ssh is given one remote command:

    sh -c SCRIPT rpshow NAME DISPLAY WAIT VERBOSE LESS-OPTIONS VIEW
        ZATHURA-SETTINGS

SCRIPT is a fixed POSIX shell program built into rpshow; the words after
it are its positional parameters, each passed in single quotes (a quote
inside a word becomes '\'').  Nothing from the command line is pasted
into SCRIPT itself, so file names with spaces, quotes or $(...) cannot
change what runs on the Pi.  NAME is the base name of FILE (with ".pdf"
added to a PDF found by its first bytes), WAIT and VERBOSE are 0 or 1.
VIEW is "pdf" for a PDF, "markdown" for a name with a Markdown
extension, else "text", and always "text" with --raw.
ZATHURA-SETTINGS holds the lines taken from the local zathurarc, plus
"set recolor false" with --light; it is empty unless VIEW is "pdf".

On the Pi the script:

 1. with VIEW=pdf, falls back to VIEW=text with a note if zathura is
    missing; otherwise stops if alacritty is missing;
 2. unsets LC_ALL (ssh forwards the local one) and LESS;
 3. makes a directory with mktemp -d under $TMPDIR or /tmp and copies
    its standard input there under NAME, so lesspipe can see the
    extension;
 4. sets LESSCHARSET=utf-8, LESSSECURE=1 and LESSSECURE_ALLOW=lessopen
    (so lesspipe still runs in secure mode, with a less that knows that
    variable: 710 does, Raspberry Pi OS 11's 551 does not) and loads
    lesspipe;
 5. tries each word of LESS-OPTIONS with the Pi's less and keeps it only
    if less prints nothing on standard error; --mouse and --MOUSE are
    always dropped;
 6. with VIEW=markdown, checks that python3 can import rich.markdown,
    and if not prints a note and falls back to plain less;
 7. with VIEW=pdf, writes a zathurarc into the directory: no status or
    input bar, "set recolor true", a right-click quit mapping in normal
    and fullscreen mode, then ZATHURA-SETTINGS;
 8. starts the window: for a PDF, "setsid zathura --mode=fullscreen"
    with the directory as its configuration, data and cache directory;
    otherwise "setsid alacritty --class rpshow" fullscreen and opaque,
    with a right-click Quit binding, running less on the copy (or, for
    Markdown, sh -c 'PYTHONIOENCODING=utf-8 python3 -m rich.markdown -c
    "$1" | less -R' with the copy as $1).  Its output goes to a log
    file in the same directory.  A background subshell that ignores
    SIGHUP starts it, records its process ID, waits for it and records
    its exit status; without WAIT it also removes the directory
    afterwards (five seconds later if the window failed, so the log can
    still be read);
 9. waits one second.  With VIEW=pdf and VERBOSE=1 it prints zathura's
    "warning:" lines from the log.  Then it looks in the log for the
    messages alacritty prints when it ignores a setting ("Ignoring
    invalid CLI option", "Unable to override option", "Config error").
    alacritty still opens a window in that case, but it may be neither
    fullscreen nor closable with a right click, and the Pi has no
    keyboard.  So the script closes the window, prints the log and
    exits 1.  This also catches errors in the Pi's own alacritty.toml;
10. with WAIT=1, waits for the window, prints the log if it failed,
    removes the directory and exits with the window's status;
11. otherwise, if the window has already failed, prints the log and
    exits with its status, else exits 0.

Tests
-----

"make check" never contacts a real host.  tests/init.sh points
RPSHOW_SSH at tests/fake-ssh, sets HOME to an empty directory and
unsets XDG_CONFIG_HOME (so your own zathurarc is not read), and puts
tests/bin, which holds a fake alacritty, zathura and python3, first in
PATH.  fake-ssh records its arguments and runs the remote command
locally with sh -c, the way sshd would, so SCRIPT really runs and the
quoting goes through two shells.  The fake alacritty records its
arguments, DISPLAY, LESS and a copy of the file it was given to show,
and exits with $FAKE_ALACRITTY_STATUS if set.  The fake zathura records
its arguments, DISPLAY, the zathurarc in its --config-dir and a copy of
the file; it prints $FAKE_ZATHURA_WARN and exits with
$FAKE_ZATHURA_STATUS if set.  The fake python3 only answers whether
rich can be imported: yes, or no when $FAKE_PYTHON_NO_RICH is set.
Each test works in its own tests/t-XXXXXX directory, used as TMPDIR for
the "remote" side, so a leftover file there means the copy was not
removed.

 - remote-test: a C unit test of src/remote.c and src/zathura.c.  It
   quotes awkward strings (single quotes, backslashes, $(...),
   backquotes, newlines, globs, the empty string, UTF-8 and SCRIPT
   itself), checks that quoted_length matches what append_quoted
   writes, and has /bin/sh print each one back to check it arrives
   unchanged.  It then has /bin/sh split commands from remote_command
   into words and checks each word: sh, -c, SCRIPT, rpshow and the
   seven arguments.  It also checks which names markdown_name and
   pdf_name take, which first bytes pdf_header takes, and which
   zathurarc lines zathura_setting passes on (comments, quoting,
   unknown options, mappings, include and values with $(...), quotes
   or backquotes).  Exit status 99 means the test itself could not run.
 - help-version.sh: --help and --version succeed without running ssh.
 - usage.sh: missing or extra operands, a missing file, a directory, a
   bad option and an empty host fail without running ssh.
 - show.sh: awkward file names arrive intact with their base name;
   host, display and less options are passed; unsupported options and
   --mouse are dropped; $LESS and $RPSHOW_HOST are used; the detached
   mode cleans up after itself.
 - markdown.sh: Markdown files (any case of the extension, awkward
   names) are run through rich and less -R; with a real python3 that
   has rich, the recorded window command is run with cat as less and
   must print coloured, rendered text; --raw, a missing rich and a
   non-Markdown file give plain less.
 - pdf.sh: PDFs (any case of the extension, awkward names, or no
   extension but "%PDF-" first) reach zathura intact with the expected
   zathurarc and options and without alacritty; a hostile zathurarc
   runs nothing; --light, $XDG_CONFIG_HOME, a missing and an unreadable
   zathurarc, --raw, a missing zathura, a failing zathura and -v
   warnings behave as documented.
 - fail.sh: a terminal that fails to start is reported with its log
   and exit status, with and without -w; a terminal that complains
   about its settings is closed and reported with status 1; a missing
   ssh gives 127.

Building
--------

    autoreconf -i     # only when building from the repository
    ./configure
    make
    make check
    make install

See INSTALL for generic instructions.  Report bugs to the address
printed by "rpshow --help".

